      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "Even with minimal store overhead on arm64, wiping `l` does not establish a security boundary. In optimized builds, `scalar_mul` inlines both multiplication and reduction into a frame where intermediate limbs, carries, and reduction temporaries spill across multiple stack slots and volatile registers (`r8-r15` on x86_64, `x0-x30` on arm64). Zeroing the 64-byte `l` array leaves those spilled temporaries and register state unscrubbed on return, while the caller still holds both inputs and the reduced output.\n\nInner-loop primitives cannot clean registers or caller frames, and they have no way to distinguish secret scalars from purely public ones in verification and ecmult. That is why libsecp256k1 enforces secret zeroization at the caller boundary—where the confidentiality context is actually known, as commit 1 does for `e`. Adding partial scratch zeroing inside arithmetic primitives introduces compiler barriers and maintenance baggage without providing real stack sanitization.\n\nCommit 1 is a clean, genuine fix; dropping commit 2 keeps the library's layer boundaries intact.\n",
